From 0b4887d00d730d755d27e7b7810982f2ed375d63 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Mathias Heider <mathias.heider@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 24 Aug 2026 02:44:05 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Load a .env locally so the agent runs outside a Space

The Space injects its secrets into the environment; a local checkout has
nothing doing that, so a clone failed with a bare 'Postgres config
incomplete' (or a psycopg traceback in the UI) even with a .env sitting next
to app.py -- nothing ever read that file.

- envfile.py: stdlib-only .env loader. Never overrides an existing
  environment variable, so Space secrets stay authoritative. Handles the
  ways a .env actually arrives on Windows: .env.txt, UTF-16 from PowerShell
  redirects, BOMs, CRLF, 'export ' prefixes, quoted values, inline comments.
  'python envfile.py' prints a masked self-check.
- agent/config.py: loads it, and exposes missing_db_vars()/setup_hint().
- agent/ui_common.py: unconfigured DB now shows what is missing and where it
  goes, instead of a psycopg traceback.
- pg_mirror.py: the same, for CLI use.
- local_bootstrap.py: builds an empty schema on a local Postgres, so a dev
  never needs the shared RDS credentials. Refuses a non-local host.
- .env.example + LOCAL_SETUP.md; .gitignore covers .env.* but keeps the
  example.

Verified: full test_e2e.py cycle green against a local Postgres configured
only by a .env; AppTest shows the actionable error with no config; and with
no .env present and secrets in the environment the app boots exactly as it
does on HF.
